"To solve the problem of finding the **maximum total path sum** in a triangle, we can use **dynamic programming**. The idea is to start from the bottom of the triangle and work our way up, updating the maximum sum at each level.\n\n### Explanation:\n- Each element in the triangle can be part of a path that goes either to the left or the right in the next row.\n- We can use a DP array where `dp[i][j]` represents the maximum path sum from the bottom of the triangle to the element at row `i`, column `j`.\n\n### Steps:\n1. Start from the bottom row and move up.\n2. For each element, the maximum path sum is the value of the element plus the maximum of the two possible paths from the next row.\n3. The final answer is the maximum path sum starting from the top of the triangle.\n\n### Python Code:\n\n```python\ndef max_path_sum(triangle, n_rows, n_cols):\n    # Start from the bottom row\n    dp = [0] * n_cols\n    for i in range(n_rows - 1, -1, -1):\n        for j in range(n_cols):\n            if i == n_rows - 1:\n                dp[j] = triangle[i][j]\n            else:\n                dp[j] = triangle[i][j] + max(dp[j], dp[j + 1])\n    return dp[0]\n\n# Test cases\nassert max_path_sum([[1, 0, 0], [4, 8, 0], [1, 5, 3]], 3, 3) == 14\nassert max_path_sum([[13, 0, 0], [7, 4, 0], [2, 4, 6]], 3, 3) == 24\nassert max_path_sum([[2, 0, 0], [11, 18, 0], [21, 25, 33]], 3, 3) == 53\n```\n\n### Explanation of the Test Cases:\n\n1. **First Test Case:**\n   ```\n   Triangle:\n   [1, 0, 0]\n   [4, 8, 0]\n   [1, 5, 3]\n   ```\n   The maximum path is `1 -> 8 -> 5` (sum = 14).\n\n2. **Second Test Case:**\n   ```\n"
