"To solve the problem, we need to:\n\n1. Split the input string into words.\n2. Check each word to see if it has an **even length**.\n3. Among those words, find the one with the **maximum length**.\n4. If no such word exists, return `\"-1\"`.\n5. If multiple words have the same maximum even length, return the **first one** that appears.\n\nHere is the Python function that does this:\n\n```python\ndef find_Max_Len_Even(s):\n    words = s.split()\n    max_len = -1\n    result = \"-1\"\n    \n    for word in words:\n        if len(word) % 2 == 0:\n            if len(word) > max_len:\n                max_len = len(word)\n                result = word\n    return result\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n\n- `s.split()` splits the input string into a list of words.\n- We iterate through each word.\n- If the word's length is even, we check if it's longer than the current `max_len`.\n- If it is, we update `max_len` and `result`.\n- If no even-length word is found, `result` remains `\"-1\"`.\n\n### Test Cases:\n\n```python\nassert find_Max_Len_Even(\"python language\") == \"language\"\nassert find_Max_Len_Even(\"maximum even length\") == \"length\"\nassert find_Max_Len_Even(\"eve\") == \"-1\"\n```\n\nAll test cases should now pass."
"To find the **index of the last occurrence** of a given number in a **sorted array**, we can use a **binary search** approach. Since the array is sorted, we can optimize the search to find the last occurrence efficiently.\n\nHere's the Python function:\n\n```python\ndef find_last_occurrence(arr, target):\n    left, right = 0, len(arr) - 1\n    result = -1\n\n    while left <= right:\n        mid = (left + right) // 2\n        if arr[mid] == target:\n            result = mid\n            left = mid + 1  # Continue searching in the right half\n        elif arr[mid] < target:\n            left = mid + 1\n        else:\n            right = mid - 1\n\n    return result\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n- We use **binary search** to efficiently locate the target.\n- When `arr[mid] == target`, we update `result` and continue searching in the **right half** to find the **last occurrence**.\n- The loop continues until `left > right`, and we return the `result`.\n\n### Test Cases:\n\n```python\nassert find_last_occurrence([2, 5, 5, 5, 6, 6, 8, 9, 9, 9], 5) == 3\nassert find_last_occurrence([2, 3, 5, 8, 6, 6, 8, 9, 9, 9], 9) == 9\nassert find_last_occurrence([2, 2, 1, 5, 6, 6, 6, 9, 9, 9], 6) == 6\n```\n\n### Output:\nAll test cases should pass, and the function will correctly return the index of the **last occurrence** of the target in the sorted array."
