"Here's a Python function that matches a word at the end of a string, allowing for optional punctuation (like a period, comma, or question mark). The function uses regular expressions to achieve this.\n\n### Explanation:\n- The pattern `r'\\b\\w+\\.\\?$'` matches:\n  - `\\b` \u2014 word boundary (to ensure the word is at the end).\n  - `\\w+` \u2014 one or more word characters (the word).\n  - `\\.?$` \u2014 an optional period or question mark (optional punctuation at the end).\n\n### Function:\n```python\nimport re\n\ndef text_match_word(text):\n    pattern = r'\\b\\w+\\.\\?$'\n    if re.search(pattern, text):\n        return 'Found a match!'\n    else:\n        return 'Not matched!'\n```\n\n### Test Cases:\n```python\nassert text_match_word(\"python.\") == 'Found a match!'\nassert text_match_word(\"python.\") == 'Found a match!'\nassert text_match_word(\"  lang  .\") == 'Not matched!'\n```\n\n### Notes:\n- The second test is a duplicate of the first, which is allowed in Python.\n- The third test fails because the word \"lang\" is not at the end of the string (there's whitespace after it).\n\nThis function should now work as expected. Let me know if you want to support more punctuation or different word boundaries."
