"Here's a Python function that checks whether a given string is a **binary string** (i.e., contains only the characters `'0'` and `'1'`):\n\n```python\ndef check(s):\n    # A binary string can only contain '0' and '1'\n    if all(c in {'0', '1'} for c in s):\n        return \"Yes\"\n    else:\n        return \"No\"\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n- The function uses a generator expression inside the `all()` function to check if **every character** in the string `s` is either `'0'` or `'1'`.\n- If all characters are valid, it returns `\"Yes\"`, otherwise `\"No\"`.\n\n### Test Cases:\n\n```python\nassert check(\"01010101010\") == \"Yes\"\nassert check(\"name0\") == \"No\"\nassert check(\"101\") == \"Yes\"\n```\n\nAll of these test cases should pass with this function."
