"To find the **common divisors** of two numbers, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. Find the **GCD (Greatest Common Divisor)** of the two numbers.\n2. Find all the **divisors** of the GCD, as those will be the common divisors of the original two numbers.\n\nHere's the Python function that does this:\n\n```python\ndef num_comm_div(a, b):\n    def gcd(x, y):\n        while y:\n            x, y = y, x % y\n        return x\n\n    def get_divisors(n):\n        if n == 0:\n            return []\n        divisors = []\n        for i in range(1, int(n**0.5) + 1):\n            if n % i == 0:\n                divisors.append(i)\n                if i != n // i:\n                    divisors.append(n // i)\n        return sorted(divisors)\n\n    g = gcd(a, b)\n    return get_divisors(g)\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n\n- `gcd(a, b)` computes the greatest common divisor of `a` and `b`.\n- `get_divisors(g)` finds all the divisors of the GCD, which are the common divisors of the original two numbers.\n\n### Test Cases:\n\n```python\nassert num_comm_div(2, 4) == [1, 2]\nassert num_comm_div(2, 8) == [1, 2]\nassert num_comm_div(12, 24) == [1, 2, 3, 6]\n```\n\n### Note:\nThe function returns a list of common divisors in sorted order. If you want the **maximum common divisor**, you can simply return `gcd(a, b)` instead of the list of divisors."
